title The cores, the rooms, and the serial pass
prompt Keaton, mid-round: let's see if we are maximizing our eight core pier server cpu value; and I still see .cursor and .vscode not retired, archived, molt breached, mitra shed prepped.
rota none -- this lap answered two questions asked mid-round rather than opening a rota row; the Fire row was read on the lap that preceded it at 20260907.011322. Its second half then took a law door the roster opened.
think The CPU question deserved a measurement rather than an impression, so I took three load samples, the user/system split, the runnable count, and the identity of every top consumer before saying anything.
think The first suspicious reading dissolved on inspection. A process named topology sat at a full core; it is copal's own comlink witness running its selftest. The two heaviest were Zig build-exe runs from bakery and diffuser, one at 293% because Zig parallelises internally. Real work, not waste.
obs THE CORES ARE FULL AND THAT IS PRECISELY BECAUSE THE LATENCY IS BAD. Load 8-10 on 8 cores, 17% idle. Underneath: the standing roster runs 161 guards strictly serially -- zero backgrounded invocations -- for 945 seconds, so each ship spends nearly sixteen minutes of every lap on ONE core while its siblings fill the other seven. Eight ships each waiting on a single-threaded pass is a way of using eight cores, and it is not the best one. 2.1 core-hours per full-fleet round.
obs AND THE SERIALISATION IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN LAZY, WHICH THE TREE'S OWN REDS PROVE. A pen one path wide for eight ships, and a fixed UDP port two runs cannot both bind, were both booked this week by peers. So parallelism is earned per guard -- a unique pen and an ephemeral port -- rather than declared. I wrote the falsifier down: if four parallel slots do not cut 945s materially, the pass is dominated by a few slow guards rather than their number, and sow at 230s makes that genuinely possible.
obs THE TWO EDITOR ROOMS ARE TWO DIFFERENT ACTS AND CALLING THEM ONE WOULD HAVE BEEN THE ERROR. .vscode is one file, no guard names it, no living citer -- retired outright this lap, with its two .gitignore un-ignores and its manifest rows. .cursor is 53 files bound by three checkable things: rule_twin sits at custody gate %7, 25 rules end with a Canonical Cursor twin footer that goes false the moment its partner does, and the seed ships the room scrubbed today. Prepped as Class M with the cut RED, because gate %7 is Keaton's word.
obs MY OWN FOLD BROKE THE LINK RULE THE GUARD I RELY ON EXISTS TO CATCH. Folding the vendor-name prep to a shelf left its links at the depth of the page they came from; the reach scan named it, and a peer shelf carried three more of exactly the same shape. Four corrections, one rule: a shelf sits a level deeper than the pin, so every climbing link gains a level.
obs A GUARD WAS REDDENING EIGHT SHIPS EVERY LAP FOR A FAULT NO LAP MAY REPAIR. %530 stands bound to two stamps on the ANOINTED SPINE ITSELF -- both folded to shelves, both published -- so derived-spine rule 3 holds for each and neither may move. A peer booked the deadlock cleanly at 20260907.014654 and left it there, correctly, because it is Keaton's word. What nobody had done is stop it costing the fleet: every ship's local spine is a COPY of the anointed one, so every ship reads the same red on every lap.
obs SO THE SCAN NOW SEPARATES WHAT A LAP CAN FIX FROM WHAT IT CANNOT, AND GATES ONLY THE FIRST. published_doubles is counted, named with both stamps and the booking, and gated nowhere; rebindings and double_booked keep their teeth on a number THIS tree bound differently, which a lap repairs by renumbering its own unshared row. A gate that reds on what nobody may touch is a gate somebody turns off -- and it changes no published byte.
obs THE CITATION COUNT DOES NOT DECIDE THE DEADLOCK, WHICH IS WORTH RECORDING BEFORE THE WORD COMES. %530 is cited in 12 files, 3 of them commit bodies that can never be edited; the earlier stamp is cited in 10 files and the later in 12. Near-balanced. What the law already supplies is the principle rather than the arithmetic: the key is the stamp, the number is a view, and a view bound twice has stopped being usable for that pair.
